# Continuous controls and alternatives

Use continuous controls when a person needs to adjust a meaningful range. Preserve the native control when it already supplies the right keyboard, touch and accessible-value behaviour. A decorative dial is not a substitute for a usable input.

The [Sliders and precise values component](../components/sliders.html) owns the reusable range, exact-entry and step-button appearance, with five complete specimens. This pattern owns the native capability, commit and reconciliation contract. The slider is a local civica.uk extension; the [UK systems review](../community/uk-design-system-review.html) records the official guidance and catalogue evidence.

**HA-CTRL-003 — reference design specification.** This specifies the local design coverage requested by HA-GAP-003 for an existing Home Assistant capability. The examples do not install a native adapter, connect to a backend or operate equipment.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-003-capabilities">Task and capability contract</h2>

| Domain input | Contract |
| --- | --- |
| Minimum, maximum and step | Use the integration's range and increments; allow decimal steps. Do not reuse a brightness percentage range for temperature or position. |
| Current value versus draft | Label the draft separately until the native contract confirms the change. |
| Unit and format | Give a visible unit and a meaningful accessible value, such as “19.5 degrees Celsius”, not an unlabelled number. |
| Commit behaviour | Decide from the native component whether changes are sent while dragging or on commit. Do not add a second subscription that duplicates requests. |
| Essential hold/double-tap action | Provide an ordinary visible control or native details route to the same task. |

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-003-states">States, pending work and failure</h2>

Retain valid minimum and maximum values; do not use a minimum to stand for unknown. When a value is missing, replace the active numeric interaction with an explicit explanation and retain the device name. If the integration changes its supported range while a person edits, preserve the draft for review and explain the new constraint rather than silently sending a clamped value.

For rapidly updating controls, distinguish a local draft from incoming reported values. Do not fight the pointer by resetting the thumb on every unrelated update. A failed commit must show the latest reported value and the unsent or unconfirmed draft honestly. A physical switch changing the value during an edit requires reconciliation, not a claim that the local draft won.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-003-content">Content and interpretation</h2>

Write “Brightness”, “Target temperature” or “Volume” with the device context. Use on/off words alongside switch state. Provide a number-entry or step-control route when precision matters and the integration supports it. Do not expose several equivalent inputs that silently disagree. The local percentage example updates only its draft; it is not a live dimmer.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-003-accessibility">Accessibility and interaction</h2>

Check arrow, Home and End behaviour against the chosen native control; do not promise shortcuts that the actual component lacks. Keep visible focus, large touch targets and a label outside a moving thumb. Preserve a slider's semantic bounds and accessible value. At 320px, 200% text and forced colours, the label and current value must remain clear. Respect circular or pill geometry where it conveys the control; strong focus and input boundaries remain distinct from quiet card dividers.

<h2 id="ha-ctrl-003-integration">Native integration boundary</h2>

The native slider and card-action contracts are the starting point. The source review recorded `ha-control-slider` at frontend commit `b1164739c3a853a28ba1c425415e96a17eb6286e`; that review does not establish compatibility for every installed frontend. A new private adapter needs its own versioned tests. The HTML form examples demonstrate labels and constraints, not replacement Home Assistant components.

